1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:10,707
Every evening, for a little over a century, someone
in a city like this did exactly what she's doing:

2
00:00:10,871 --> 00:00:17,470
walked out at dusk with a ladder and a long pole, and
gave the street its night.

3
00:00:18,947 --> 00:00:21,257
IDA: Evening, old street.

4
00:00:22,947 --> 00:00:24,249
Ida isn't real.

5
00:00:24,438 --> 00:00:26,402
Her city has no name.

6
00:00:26,572 --> 00:00:31,476
But her job was real, and so are the numbers you're
about to hear.

7
00:00:31,953 --> 00:00:34,469
This is a story about that job:

8
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:42,862
how it began, how fast it grew, how it ended, and the
handful of people who still do it.

9
00:00:43,053 --> 00:00:47,925
And at the end, a question about the jobs we do
today.

10
00:00:51,593 --> 00:00:57,418
Our story starts in London, in 1807, on a single
street:

11
00:00:57,597 --> 00:00:58,538
Pall Mall.

12
00:00:58,704 --> 00:01:04,670
An inventor named Frederick Winsor ran pipes along it
and lit a row of lamps burning coal gas.

13
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:08,429
It is remembered as the first street in the world lit
that way.

14
00:01:08,894 --> 00:01:11,532
People came out just to stare at it.

15
00:01:11,994 --> 00:01:17,135
In 1812 the world's first gas company received its
royal charter.

16
00:01:17,304 --> 00:01:20,737
A year later, Westminster Bridge was glowing.

17
00:01:21,199 --> 00:01:25,253
And then the thing spread the way good ideas and bad
smells do:

18
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:26,106
quickly.

19
00:01:26,272 --> 00:01:35,008
By the 1820s, London had more than forty thousand gas
lamps along some two hundred and fifteen miles of
street.

20
00:01:35,494 --> 00:01:36,852
Forty thousand.

21
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:40,335
Hold on to that number; we'll come back to it.

22
00:01:40,819 --> 00:01:44,016
Forty thousand lamps don't light themselves.

23
00:01:44,198 --> 00:01:46,543
Every one of them needed a person.

24
00:01:47,207 --> 00:01:50,019
A lamplighter's evening was called a round.

25
00:01:50,189 --> 00:01:58,942
You lit dozens of lamps at dusk, one after another
along a fixed route, then walked the same route at
dawn to put them out.

26
00:01:59,438 --> 00:02:02,360
In between, the job was more than fire.

27
00:02:02,523 --> 00:02:12,497
You cleaned the glass, reported the broken ones, and
knew, without a map, which corner always went out
first in the wind.

28
00:02:12,969 --> 00:02:15,501
Rain, fog, frost:

29
00:02:15,685 --> 00:02:16,945
it didn't matter.

30
00:02:17,103 --> 00:02:23,652
If the lamps on your round weren't lit by the time
people noticed the dark, people noticed you.

31
00:02:24,126 --> 00:02:30,253
Forty thousand lamps meant a small army walking the
streets every dusk and every dawn.

32
00:02:30,926 --> 00:02:33,683
The idea crossed the Atlantic fast.

33
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:42,545
In 1816 a painter, Rembrandt Peale, lit a room of his
Baltimore museum with gas, as a kind of show.

34
00:02:42,712 --> 00:02:51,247
Within a year, on the seventh of February 1817,
Baltimore lit America's first public gas street lamp.

35
00:02:51,722 --> 00:02:54,091
Paris took its time and then went all in.

36
00:02:54,251 --> 00:02:57,816
By the middle of the century its boulevards ran on
gas.

37
00:03:00,496 --> 00:03:02,754
Then came a different kind of light.

38
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:13,009
In 1878, for the World's Fair, Paris strung
sixty-four electric arc lamps along and around the
Avenue de l'Opéra.

39
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:18,033
They were harsh and blue-white, and they did not need
anybody on a ladder.

40
00:03:18,498 --> 00:03:24,366
Two years later, in 1880, New York put electric
lights on Broadway.

41
00:03:24,828 --> 00:03:26,916
At first, gas held on.

42
00:03:27,084 --> 00:03:31,854
Electricity was new, expensive, sometimes unreliable.

43
00:03:32,022 --> 00:03:39,316
But each white lamp that went up was one less stop on
somebody's round, and the people doing the rounds
could count.

44
00:03:40,591 --> 00:03:43,621
IDA: Not yet… not yet.

45
00:03:44,791 --> 00:03:48,079
It was a slow tide, not a single night:

46
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:55,687
a white lamp on the main avenue one year, a few more
on the side streets the next, until the round got
shorter.

47
00:03:56,155 --> 00:04:00,337
Ida's watch runs seven minutes slow, and she knows
it.

48
00:04:00,514 --> 00:04:02,287
That's the feeling we wanted:

49
00:04:02,457 --> 00:04:07,253
the city's clock moving on, and someone still walking
on the old time.

50
00:04:07,911 --> 00:04:13,097
In April 1907, New York's lamplighters went on
strike.

51
00:04:13,256 --> 00:04:22,981
They worked for the private company that lit the
streets that still had no electric lamps, and they
wanted their union recognised and better pay.

52
00:04:23,446 --> 00:04:28,170
The next morning's papers reported parts of the city
in darkness.

53
00:04:28,358 --> 00:04:36,154
Police officers were sent out to light the lamps
themselves, some of them climbing the posts with
matches.

54
00:04:36,627 --> 00:04:40,093
Within about a week, the men were back at work.

55
00:04:40,555 --> 00:04:42,865
But the strike made something plain.

56
00:04:43,034 --> 00:04:46,801
A strike only works if the city can't do without you.

57
00:04:46,977 --> 00:04:51,385
And every year, the city could do a little more
without them.

58
00:04:52,051 --> 00:04:54,262
Here's the part most people don't know.

59
00:04:54,417 --> 00:04:56,474
London still has gas street lamps:

60
00:04:56,647 --> 00:05:01,280
in Covent Garden, along the Mall, outside palaces and
theatres.

61
00:05:01,742 --> 00:05:05,237
In 2015, there were about fifteen hundred of them.

62
00:05:05,398 --> 00:05:11,406
By British Gas's own count in 2023, its team looks
after about eleven hundred.

63
00:05:11,865 --> 00:05:13,579
And that team is five people.

64
00:05:13,743 --> 00:05:15,045
Five lamplighters.

65
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,439
They don't carry fire anymore.

66
00:05:17,601 --> 00:05:24,579
Most of the lamps run on small clockwork timers, and
every two weeks someone climbs up to wind the clock
and check the lamp.

67
00:05:25,032 --> 00:05:31,487
When Westminster proposed swapping its gas lamps for
LEDs in 2022, residents objected.

68
00:05:31,648 --> 00:05:39,060
In 2024, four lamps in Covent Garden, put up in 1910,
were formally listed as protected heritage.

69
00:05:39,527 --> 00:05:41,710
Forty thousand lamps, once.

70
00:05:41,869 --> 00:05:43,344
About eleven hundred now.

71
00:05:43,503 --> 00:05:44,455
Five people.

72
00:05:45,116 --> 00:05:50,378
We gave Ida an ending the real lamplighters mostly
didn't get.

73
00:05:50,551 --> 00:05:53,316
She gets to choose her last lamp.

74
00:05:53,805 --> 00:06:02,934
For the real ones, it was a new contract, or a white
post on the route one morning, and the round was
gone.

75
00:06:03,097 --> 00:06:06,324
History doesn't record most of their names.

76
00:06:16,808 --> 00:06:19,383
What Ida keeps isn't the job.

77
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:24,647
It's the small warm thing the job was for, and she
hands it on.

78
00:06:25,133 --> 00:06:27,412
Cas will never light a street.

79
00:06:27,599 --> 00:06:30,113
He'll grow up in the white city.

80
00:06:30,288 --> 00:06:37,752
But he knows how to warm his hands at a lamp and
count to three, because somebody showed him.

81
00:06:46,406 --> 00:06:48,550
Lamplighting was an extreme case:

82
00:06:48,713 --> 00:06:52,989
a job that was almost entirely one task, and a switch
could do it.

83
00:06:53,451 --> 00:07:02,362
The economist James Bessen took about two hundred and
seventy occupations from the 1950 U.S. Census and
followed them to 2010.

84
00:07:02,826 --> 00:07:08,601
Plenty disappeared, often because people stopped
needing them, like telegraph operators.

85
00:07:08,757 --> 00:07:12,153
But only one vanished mainly because a machine took
over the work:

86
00:07:12,316 --> 00:07:14,004
the elevator operator.

87
00:07:14,463 --> 00:07:16,421
One in two hundred and seventy.

88
00:07:16,583 --> 00:07:20,111
Machines mostly took tasks, not whole jobs.

89
00:07:20,775 --> 00:07:22,599
So who is on that list today?

90
00:07:23,048 --> 00:07:29,946
Every year, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics
projects how American jobs will change over the next
ten years.

91
00:07:30,086 --> 00:07:36,642
The newest, published in August 2026, cover 2025 to
2035.

92
00:07:37,104 --> 00:07:39,048
Top of the fastest-shrinking list:

93
00:07:39,176 --> 00:07:44,168
word processors and typists, projected to fall by
about thirty-four percent.

94
00:07:44,329 --> 00:07:51,016
Then telephone operators, down nearly twenty-eight
percent; only about three and a half thousand are
left.

95
00:07:51,152 --> 00:07:55,524
Switchboard operators and data entry keyers, each
down about a quarter.

96
00:07:55,675 --> 00:07:58,122
Telemarketers, down about a fifth.

97
00:07:58,550 --> 00:07:59,752
What they share:

98
00:07:59,895 --> 00:08:05,714
much of the work is one repeated task, and automation
tools, including AI, are taking on more of it.

99
00:08:05,855 --> 00:08:08,482
These are forecasts, not verdicts.

100
00:08:08,611 --> 00:08:10,225
But the pattern sounds familiar.

101
00:08:10,869 --> 00:08:18,728
In an earlier round, for 2023 to 2033, BLS spelled
out where it expects AI to reduce demand:

102
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:24,802
customer service representatives down five percent,
auto damage appraisers down about nine.

103
00:08:24,956 --> 00:08:27,563
But software developers up almost eighteen.

104
00:08:27,715 --> 00:08:30,605
AI changes work; it doesn't only remove it.

105
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:33,769
And the newest list has fast-growing jobs too:

106
00:08:33,926 --> 00:08:41,952
nurse practitioners, solar installers, data
scientists, wind turbine technicians, each up about
thirty percent or more.

107
00:08:42,410 --> 00:08:43,640
One early warning.

108
00:08:43,795 --> 00:08:58,572
Stanford's Digital Economy Lab found that, by June
2026, employment of workers aged twenty-two to
twenty-five in the jobs most exposed to AI was about
nineteen percent below where it would otherwise be,
mostly because fewer were hired.

109
00:08:58,730 --> 00:09:01,065
Experienced workers showed no such gap.

110
00:09:01,526 --> 00:09:05,400
The researchers call it an early signal, not proof
that AI caused it.

111
00:09:06,058 --> 00:09:10,657
Forty thousand lamps became about eleven hundred, and
a team of five.

112
00:09:10,818 --> 00:09:14,667
Out of two hundred and seventy jobs, one disappeared
outright.

113
00:09:14,837 --> 00:09:17,531
Today, some rounds are getting shorter:

114
00:09:17,688 --> 00:09:21,059
typists, projected down thirty-four percent.

115
00:09:21,517 --> 00:09:22,670
Some jobs end.

116
00:09:22,827 --> 00:09:24,197
Most change.

117
00:09:24,356 --> 00:09:27,094
And new ones appear that nobody had a name for yet.

118
00:09:27,863 --> 00:09:30,293
IDA (distant): Evening, old street.

119
00:09:30,563 --> 00:09:33,830
So, the question this channel will keep asking:

120
00:09:34,295 --> 00:09:36,815
Who are the last lamplighters of our time?
